      "text": "To find the projection matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) that projects any vector onto the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we first need to determine the projection matrix when the vector is normalized. The vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\) can be normalized by dividing by its magnitude, which is \\(\\sqrt{(-3)^2 + (-2)^2} = \\sqrt{13}\\). Therefore, the normalized vector is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nThe projection matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) onto a vector \\(\\mathbf{a} = \\begin{pmatrix} a_1 \\\\ a_2 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{P} = \\frac{\\mathbf{a} \\mathbf{a}^T}{\\mathbf{a}^T \\mathbf{a}}\n\\]\n\nHere, \\(\\mathbf{a} = \\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\end{pmatrix}\\). First, we calculate \\(\\mathbf{a} \\mathbf{a}^T\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\mathbf{a}^T = \\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\left(-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\right)^2 & \\left(-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\right) \\left(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\right) \\\\ \\left(-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\right) \\left(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\right) & \\left(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\right)^2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{9}{13} & \\frac{6}{13} \\\\ \\frac{6}{13} & \\frac{4}{13} \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate \\(\\mathbf{a}^T \\mathbf{a}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a}^T \\mathbf{a} = \\left(-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\right)^2 + \\left(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\right)^2 = \\frac{9}{13} + \\frac{4}{13} = \\frac{13}{13} = 1\n\\]\n\nThus, the projection matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{P} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{9}{13} & \\frac{6}{13} \\\\ \\frac{6}{13} & \\frac{4}{13} \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nTo find the inverse of \\(\\mathbf{P}\\), we use the formula for the inverse of a \\(2 \\times 2\\) matrix \\(\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix}\\), which is \\(\\frac{1}{ad - bc} \\begin{pmatrix} d & -b \\\\ -c & a \\end{pmatrix}\\). Here, \\(a = \\frac{9}{13}\\), \\(b = \\frac{6}{13}\\), \\(c = \\frac{6}{13}\\), and \\(d = \\frac{4}{13}\\). The determinant of \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\det(\\mathbf{P}) = \\left(\\frac{9}{13}\\right)\\left(\\frac{4}{13}\\right) - \\left(\\frac{6}{13}\\right)\\left(\\frac{6}{13}\\right) = \\frac{36}{169} - \\frac{36}{169} = 0\n\\]\n\nSince the determinant is zero, the inverse of \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) does not exist. Therefore, we enter the zero matrix:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]",
